A two-index comprehension `[f(i, j) for i in ..., j in ...]` builds a matrix directly with the element type inferred from `f`. In `vandermonde`, `xi^j` keeps integers integers, and the columns are powers 0 to `deg`. Base's `I(n)` is a lazy identity that is usually better than a materialized one.

```julia
function word_freq(text::AbstractString, n::Integer = 3)
    counts = Dict{String,Int}()
    for m in eachmatch(r"\p{L}+", text)
        w = lowercase(m.match)
        counts[w] = get(counts, w, 0) + 1
    end
    ranked = sort!(collect(counts); by = p -> (-last(p), first(p)))
    return first(ranked, n)
end

# Checks
text = "The cat and the hat. The CAT sat; and sat, and sat!"
@assert word_freq(text) == ["and" => 3, "sat" => 3, "the" => 3]
@assert word_freq(text, 2) == ["and" => 3, "sat" => 3]
@assert word_freq("", 5) == Pair{String,Int}[]
@assert word_freq("Über über ÜBER x", 1) == ["über" => 3]
```

`\p{L}+` matches runs of letters in any script, so punctuation and digits act as separators. Ties are broken alphabetically through the sort key `(-count, word)`, making the output deterministic even though `Dict` order is not. `first(ranked, n)` returns fewer than `n` items when there are not that many distinct words.

