Two defects. In `"$name[$i] = $v[i]"`, interpolation takes the identifier `v` alone, so each line printed the entire vector followed by the literal text `[i]`; an element access must be wrapped as `$(v[i])`. The name is wrapped too (`$(name)[...]`) so the following `[` can never be read as indexing. Second, `1:length(v)` assumes 1-based indices: with an `OffsetArray` it indexes out of range or labels the wrong elements, while `eachindex(v)` yields whatever valid indices the array has. Returning a `String` rather than printing makes the function testable; callers can `print(report(...))`.

```julia
function is_armstrong(n::Integer)
    d = digits(n)
    return n == sum(x -> x^length(d), d)
end

# Checks
@assert is_armstrong(153) && is_armstrong(9474) && is_armstrong(5)
@assert !is_armstrong(154)
@assert filter(is_armstrong, 100:999) == [153, 370, 371, 407]
@assert filter(is_armstrong, 1:9999) == [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 153, 370, 371, 407, 1634, 8208, 9474]
```

A number is Armstrong if the sum of its digits each raised to the number of digits equals the number itself. Computing `digits` once, rather than converting to a string, avoids parsing and keeps the code integer-only. For very large n the digit powers could overflow `Int`; convert with `big` first in that case.

```julia
poly_eval(coeffs, x) = evalpoly(x, coeffs)

function poly_deriv_coeffs(coeffs::AbstractVector)
    length(coeffs) <= 1 && return zero(coeffs)[1:min(1, end)]
    return [(k - 1) * coeffs[k] for k in firstindex(coeffs)+1:lastindex(coeffs)]
end

# Checks
p = [1, 2, 3]                       # 1 + 2x + 3x^2
@assert poly_eval(p, 2) == 17
@assert poly_eval(p, 1 + 1im) == 1 + 2 * (1 + 1im) + 3 * (1 + 1im)^2
@assert poly_eval(poly_deriv_coeffs(p), 2) == 14
@assert evalpoly(2, (1, 2, 3)) == 17         # tuples unroll at compile time
```

`evalpoly` uses Horner's scheme, which needs n multiplications and is more accurate than summing powers; it takes the coefficients in ascending order and works for any numeric `x`, including complex values. For a derivative the coefficient of `x^(k-2)` is `(k-1) * c_k`, with indices starting at one. A tuple of coefficients lets the compiler unroll the evaluation.

